HOME_MORE = [
("how-to-unblock-a-toilet", "guide",
"How to unblock a toilet, calmly",
"The physics-first method: water pressure beats panic, chemicals are last, and the signs that say call a plumber.",
"""<p>A blocked toilet is a five-minute problem wearing a disaster costume. The fix is almost always water pressure applied patiently — and the main skill is knowing when to stop pushing.</p>
<h2>First: stop flushing</h2>
<p>If the water sits high, do not flush again — the bowl only holds one more flush. Give it ten minutes; a partially blocked drain often drains down on its own, and a lower water level makes every method safer and less splashy.</p>
<h2>The method: hot water, then the plunger</h2>
<p>Pour in a bucket of warm (not boiling — boiling can crack the porcelain) water from about waist height: the weight and warmth alone often shifts a paper-only blockage. Wait. Then use a plunger — ideally the flanged type, but a standard one works — sealing it over the outlet and pushing <em>gently</em> first (air compresses; the first hard push splashes back), then firmly, keeping the seal. The physics is a column of water pushing, not the rubber. Ten to twenty firm strokes, then check the water level.</p>
<h2>If that fails: the washing-up trick, then the auger</h2>
<p>A generous squirt of washing-up liquid left to sit in the warm water can lubricate the passage. Beyond that, a toilet auger (drain snake with a protective sleeve) winds past the bowl's internal trap and pulls or pushes the blockage through — hardware shops stock them cheaply, and they reach where plungers cannot. Chemical caustic cleaners are the <em>last</em> resort in a toilet: they sit in the standing water (a hazard for whoever augers next), and never mix products — the same rule as sinks.</p>
<h2>Stop and call a plumber when</h2>
<p>Water rises when another fixture drains (the blockage is downstream in the soil pipe), the toilet blocks repeatedly (something is wrong further in — or a child's toy has taken up residence), or there is sewage smell that persists after it clears. Those are drain-line problems, not bowl problems, and they only get more expensive with optimism. Everything else on this page is <a href="/how-to-fix-a-slow-draining-sink/">the same physics we used on the slow sink</a>, one storey lower.</p>"""),

("basic-toolkit-checklist", "guide",
"The basic toolkit: what a first toolbox actually needs",
"Eleven honest items, one smart upgrade, and why the giant box-of-everything set is usually the wrong first buy.",
"""<p>The first toolbox fails in one of two ways: it is a 200-piece novelty set with 190 useless pieces, or it never gets bought because the choices felt overwhelming. Here is the honest middle path — eleven items that handle almost every low-risk household job, plus the one upgrade worth saving for.</p>
<h2>The eleven</h2>
<p>A claw hammer (16oz is the honest all-rounder). Two screwdrivers — one flat-head, one cross-head — or better, one driver with a set of bits. An adjustable spanner for the nuts and fittings plumbing tasks reveal. A tape measure (5m). A small spirit level; the phone's is a backup, not a tool. A utility knife with spare blades. Pliers — combination pliers do gripping, twisting and gentle pulling. A torch (head-torch version leaves your hands free). Insulating tape and masking tape. A pencil, for marking before cutting anything. And a pack of wall plugs and a few screws and nails in common sizes — half of DIY is having the right fixing at the right moment.</p>
<h2>The upgrade: a cordless drill/driver</h2>
<p>After a month of real jobs you will know whether you need one — and if you are hanging shelves or brackets, you do. A basic cordless drill/driver from a reputable brand transforms screwdriving from wrist-work into a squeeze of a trigger. Buy the tool plus one good general-purpose bit set; skip the giant battery bundles until the tool has earned them. Drilling walls safely — knowing what is behind them — is its own discipline: our <a href="/why-does-my-circuit-breaker-keep-tripping/">breaker guide</a> explains the protection you are hoping not to meet, and the <a href="/seasonal-home-maintenance-checklist/">seasonal checklist</a> is where the toolkit earns its keep.</p>
<h2>Quality, honestly calibrated</h2>
<p>Buy the cheap version of things you will rarely use and the good version of things in your hand weekly: the hammer, the screwdriver, the tape measure. And the safety boundary is not a tool: ladders beyond a step-stool, anything electrical beyond the socket face, and gas are professional territory — the toolkit stops politely at that line.</p>"""),

("how-to-bleed-a-radiator", "guide",
"How to bleed a radiator (and why it goes cold at the top)",
"Trapped air is the usual culprit for a radiator that is hot at the bottom and cold at the top — a ten-minute fix with one pressure check at the end.",
"""<p>If your home has radiators and one is hot at the bottom but cool at the top, it is holding trapped air. Air gathers at the top of the radiator and insulates exactly the part that should be hottest. Bleeding — letting the air out — is a ten-minute job needing one cheap key and one careful check afterwards.</p>
<h2>The signs and the setup</h2>
<p>Cold at the top, warm at the bottom: air. Cold all over while neighbours on the same system are hot: a valve or system problem instead — that is not a bleeding job. To bleed, you need a radiator key (a couple of coins or a flat screwdriver fit some modern valves), a cloth, and a small bowl. The heating should be off and the radiators cool — bleeding a hot, running system sprays and sucks in more air.</p>
<h2>The bleed, step by step</h2>
<p>At one end of the radiator's top edge is the bleed valve. Fit the key, hold the cloth and bowl beneath, and turn slowly anti-clockwise about a quarter to half a turn. You will hear a hiss — that is the air leaving. When water starts to seep steadily (not spray), close it: hand-tight plus a small nudge is enough. Work around the house radiator by radiator. The commonly-suggested order is to start with the radiators furthest from the boiler or upstairs first; systems vary, and none of it is sensitive enough to fear — the point is simply to do every radiator, not just the noisy one.</p>
<h2>The step that matters afterwards: pressure</h2>
<p>Bleeding releases pressure as well as air, and boilers are fussy about theirs. After bleeding, check the boiler's pressure gauge: most sealed systems want the needle roughly in the 1 to 1.5 bar zone when cold (your boiler's manual states the exact band). If it has dropped below, repressurise using the filling loop per the manual's instructions — two minutes with a tap-like lever. If the radiator is still cold at the top after a proper bleed, or you bleed weekly, the system has a deeper issue (corrosion sludge or a failing pump) and that is a heating engineer's visit. General information, not professional advice — and the boiler manual beats any website for your exact model.</p>"""),

("washing-machine-wont-drain", "guide",
"Washing machine won't drain? Check these before you call anyone",
"The filter, the hose, the pump: the three suspects in order — with the unplug-first rule that is not negotiable.",
"""<p>A drum full of grey water and a machine that hums but does nothing is most often one of three things: a blocked filter, a kinked or blocked hose, or a failed drain pump. Two of the three are homeowner fixes. Work in order, and start with the rule that outranks everything: <b>unplug the machine before you put your hands anywhere near its innards</b>.</p>
<h2>1. The filter (the usual culprit)</h2>
<p>Behind the small hatch near the floor lives the drain filter — the trap where coins, hair clips, lint and the odd baby sock retire. Put a shallow tray and towels down, open the hatch slowly (water <em>will</em> come), and unscrew the filter. Clear it, rinse it, check the recess for debris, and screw it back snugly. Our <a href="/how-to-clean-a-washing-machine/">washing-machine cleaning guide</a> covers this as seasonal maintenance; when a machine stops draining, this filter is where the story usually ends.</p>
<h2>2. The drain hose</h2>
<p>Slide the machine out enough to see the flexible hose running to the standpipe or under-sink connection. Feel its whole length for kinks; pull it off the standpipe (have a bucket ready) and check for a blockage at the end and in the standpipe itself. Re-fit it at the height the manual states — a hose hanging too low can siphon water out mid-cycle and mimic other faults.</p>
<h2>3. Reading the pump's behaviour</h2>
<p>Start a drain/spin cycle and listen at the filter hatch. A humming-then-clicking silence: the pump is trying and blocked (recheck the filter recess and the pump impeller if your model exposes it). Total silence where the pump used to chatter: the pump or its electrical supply has likely failed — a repairable part, but that job crosses from maintenance into repair: a technician visit usually earns its fee here, especially since pumps are model-specific parts.</p>
<h2>The honest boundaries</h2>
<p>Water everywhere means working with bowls and towels, patience, and the machine pulled out only when it is safe and stable. If the door stays locked after a drain attempt, most machines release it a few minutes after power is cut — never force it mid-cycle with water standing. And any job that wants the machine's internals opened beyond the filter belongs to someone who does it weekly.</p>"""),
]

FIT_MORE = [
("walking-vs-running", "guide",
"Walking or running? The honest comparison for beginners",
"Both count, both work. The real differences — time, impact, and the one that survives your schedule — decide the winner for you.",
"""<p>The question assumes a contest. The honest answer from the guidelines themselves: both walking and running count as aerobic activity, and the best one is the one you will actually keep doing. But they do trade differently, and knowing the trade-offs makes the choice a decision instead of a guilt.</p>
<h2>The trade-offs, stated plainly</h2>
<p><b>Time:</b> running is vigorous activity, walking is moderate — per guideline-minute, running does more work in less time, which is its honest advantage for packed schedules. <b>Impact:</b> running loads the joints and tendons far harder; walking is gentle on the body and easier to sustain daily — which matters more than beginners expect, because the programme only works if tomorrow's session happens. <b>The on-ramp:</b> walking scales itself — a beginner can walk thirty minutes within weeks; running demands a walk-run build-up to arrive at the same place without the shins complaining. <b>Adherence, quietly the decider:</b> the activity you do not dread is the one that compounds. A walking habit you keep for a year beats a running plan you abandon in three weeks — and beats it decisively.</p>
<h2>The honest hybrid</h2>
<p>They are not rivals; they are gears. Many beginners build the habit walking, then introduce gentle run-walk intervals when the walks feel easy — our <a href="/30-day-walking-plan/">30-day walking plan</a> is the foundation, and its guidance about finishing each day able to do two or three more applies to running intervals too. Others stay walkers for life and meet the guidelines completely — the strength work from <a href="/strength-training-for-beginners/">the beginner strength guide</a> pairs with either.</p>
<h2>The one-line answer</h2>
<p>Choose by your week, not your ambition: short on time and healthy joints — running, built gradually. Starting from zero, heavier, or returning after a long gap — walking, unapologetically. Both are the sport; showing up is the skill.</p>"""),

("how-to-warm-up", "guide",
"How to warm up (in five minutes, without rituals)",
"What a warm-up actually does, the three-part pattern that covers every session, and where static stretching honestly belongs.",
"""<p>A warm-up is not a ceremony; it is a rehearsal. Done in five minutes, it raises your temperature, opens the joints you are about to use, and lets the first working set feel like the second. Skip it and sessions feel heavier, tighter and more accident-prone — that is the whole case, and it needs no mysticism.</p>
<h2>The three-part pattern</h2>
<p><b>One: raise.</b> Two or three minutes of easy movement — marching on the spot, a slow walk building to a brisk one, gentle cycling. You are aiming for warmth and slightly deeper breath, not fatigue. <b>Two: open.</b> A minute of dynamic moves for the joints the session will use: leg swings front and side for legs days, arm circles and torso turns for upper-body days, hip circles always. Dynamic means moving through the range — not holding. <b>Three: rehearse.</b> Do the first exercise of your session at an easy scale: half-depth squats before squats, wall push-ups before floor push-ups, a slower first five minutes of the walk. This is the part beginners skip and athletes never do.</p>
<h2>Where static stretching honestly fits</h2>
<p>The long-hold stretches you picture when someone says &ldquo;stretching&rdquo; belong mostly <em>after</em> the session, on warm muscles — as a cool-down ritual, which is worthwhile for the wind-down even though its magic-injury-prevention reputation has been softened by research. Before exercise, long static holds can slightly reduce power output and do little to prevent injury; the moving version (part two above) is the better pre-session tool. Both have a place; the order is the point.</p>
<h2>The honest minimum</h2>
<p>For a brisk walk, the warm-up is the first five minutes walked gently — our <a href="/30-day-walking-plan/">walking plan</a> bakes that in. For the <a href="/strength-training-for-beginners/">strength sessions</a>, use the full three-part pattern; it costs five minutes and buys a session that feels lighter from the first rep. General information, not medical advice — if a joint hurts during the warm-up itself, that is information worth respecting, not pushing through.</p>"""),
]

# ---- batch 18: fitness engagement batch - protein + sleep (researched 11 Sep 2026, YMYL protocol) ----
FIT_MORE.extend([
("how-much-protein-do-you-need", "guide",
"How much protein do you actually need?",
"The official number, why trainers quote bigger ones, food before powders — the honest arithmetic, with the lines this desk will not cross.",
"""<p>Protein is the fitness world's favourite argument. The honest answer is less dramatic than the supplement aisle suggests: there is an official number, it has a purpose, and the right number for <em>you</em> depends on what you are asking your body to do. This is general information, not medical or dietary advice — kidney or other health conditions change the picture, so a qualified professional is the right stop first.</p>
<h2>The official number</h2>
<p>The recommended dietary allowance for adults is <b>0.8 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day</b> — about 56 g for a 70 kg adult. That figure (set in the official RDA reviews) is what prevents deficiency for most healthy adults; it is a floor, not a target for optimisation. As a share of calories, official guidance lands protein anywhere in a broad 10–35% band.</p>
<h2>Why you will hear bigger numbers</h2>
<p>People in serious training — and older adults, whose bodies use protein less efficiently — are commonly advised to eat more than the RDA: sports-nutrition position stands typically land in a <b>rough 1.2–2.0 g/kg</b> band for hard-training people, and research summaries for older adults argue for around 1.2–1.5 g/kg to protect muscle. The logic is simple: training creates the demand; protein is the raw material the <a href="/how-progressive-overload-works/">overload principle</a> spends. None of this requires precision: within those bands, consistency beats arithmetic.</p>
<h2>Food first, powder optional</h2>
<p>For most people, normal food covers it: eggs, dairy, beans and lentils, fish, chicken, meat, tofu, even bread and oats contribute. A rough daily picture for many adults lands within range without any powder at all. Protein powder is a convenience, not an ingredient of success — useful on heavy training days or low-appetite mornings, unnecessary as an identity. Claims that a specific shake builds muscle by itself are selling, not science.</p>
<h2>The lines this desk will not cross</h2>
<p>No gram targets for people with kidney or liver conditions (professional guidance first); no "more is always better" — the RDA reviews set upper boundaries for a reason; and no promising protein alone changes a body: it works inside the pair that actually does — progressive training and enough sleep (<a href="/sleep-and-exercise-performance/">the recovery multiplier</a>). Pair the numbers with the <a href="/strength-training-for-beginners/">two-day strength habit</a> and the arithmetic becomes background noise.</p>"""),
("sleep-and-exercise-performance", "guide",
"Sleep: the recovery tool you cannot buy",
"Seven hours is the public-health floor — here is what short sleep does to your training, and the habits that survive real life.",
"""<p>The most powerful recovery tool on this desk costs nothing and is widely skipped. Adults are recommended <b>at least seven hours of sleep a night</b> (ages 18–60, per the CDC-backed recommendation) — and roughly one in three adults reports getting less. This is general information, not medical advice: persistent insomnia, snoring with gasping, or daytime exhaustion despite enough hours in bed deserve a professional conversation.</p>
<h2>What short sleep does to training</h2>
<p>The documented associations are not subtle: regularly sleeping under seven hours is linked with higher risk of obesity, high blood pressure, type 2 diabetes, heart disease and frequent mental distress. For your workouts specifically, short sleep shows up as heavier sessions, worse adherence (the <a href="/how-to-start-working-out/">two-day rule</a> dies first), and blunted recovery — the adaptation you are training for happens between sessions, and much of it is sleep-mediated. See <a href="/rest-days-and-recovery/">rest days and recovery</a> for the between-session logic.</p>
<h2>The habits that survive real life</h2>
<p>The public-health guidance is refreshingly boring: <b>same bedtime, same wake time</b> — including weekends; keep screens and their light out of the last stretch of the evening; keep the bedroom dark, quiet and cool; and treat caffeine as a morning tool with an afternoon curfew. Naps are not a moral failure — a short early-afternoon nap is a legitimate tool; long late ones borrow from the night. Shift workers have it hardest, and the honest note is that consistency of <em>schedule</em>, not the specific schedule, does most of the work.</p>
<h2>How sleep and training help each other</h2>
<p>The relationship runs both ways: people who exercise regularly generally fall asleep faster and report better sleep quality — with the classic caveat that hard sessions right before bed can delay sleep for some people; if evenings are your only slot, keep the pre-bed intensity easy (a <a href="/walking-vs-running/">conversational walk</a> rather than intervals). Pair the seven-hour floor with the <a href="/30-day-walking-plan/">30-day plan</a> and the two strength days, and you have the complete beginner system: move most days, challenge the muscles twice, sleep like it matters — because it is the part that makes the rest work.</p>"""),
])
